WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.699
Why cosine?

00:00:01.699 --> 00:00:04.973
A circle point gives a real Legendre input.

00:00:05.303 --> 00:00:07.908
Mark z on the unit circle at angle t.

00:00:07.908 --> 00:00:11.620
Its reciprocal is its mirror image across the real axis.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.317
Join the two points.

00:00:13.317 --> 00:00:18.266
Their midpoint keeps their common horizontal coordinate, x equals cosine t.

00:00:18.596 --> 00:00:20.383
The midpoint sweeps the interval.

00:00:20.383 --> 00:00:24.036
Turn from zero to pi, sweeping x from one to minus one.

00:00:24.366 --> 00:00:25.925
Stop at ninety degrees.

00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:27.335
The input is zero.

00:00:27.335 --> 00:00:31.815
The quadratic Legendre polynomial takes the value minus one half there.

00:00:32.145 --> 00:00:33.420
Read the graph.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.115
The input is zero and the height is minus one half.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.036
Give each phase point a mass.

00:00:40.036 --> 00:00:42.762
Their balance point gives the height of the graph.

00:00:43.092 --> 00:00:45.018
Degree two has three phases.

00:00:45.018 --> 00:00:48.830
Their angles are twice t, zero, and minus twice t.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.640
The weights add to one.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.007
Three eighths, one quarter, and three eighths are all positive.

00:00:55.337 --> 00:00:57.024
Set t to sixty degrees.

00:00:57.024 --> 00:00:59.415
The three phase points form a triangle.

00:00:59.415 --> 00:01:01.634
Their weighted mean is its balance point.

00:01:01.964 --> 00:01:04.206
The two mirror points have equal mass.

00:01:04.206 --> 00:01:08.814
Their imaginary parts cancel, so the balance point sits on the real axis.

00:01:09.144 --> 00:01:10.590
Read the balance point.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.692
At x equal to one half, P two takes the value minus one eighth.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.439
Why exactly one?

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:21.284
Can a Legendre mode exceed one in absolute value?

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:23.759
Every phase has modulus one.

00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:26.734
Each carries a positive weight, in every degree.

00:01:27.064 --> 00:01:29.377
Normalization gives the total weight.

00:01:29.377 --> 00:01:32.096
At angle zero, all phases equal one.

00:01:32.096 --> 00:01:34.767
The weights sum to P sub n of one, which is one.

00:01:35.097 --> 00:01:36.592
The disk is convex.

00:01:36.592 --> 00:01:41.584
It contains every weighted mean of these points, so the absolute value is at most one.

00:01:41.914 --> 00:01:44.101
The bound covers the whole interval.

00:01:44.101 --> 00:01:48.399
Every x from minus one to one is cosine t for some angle.

00:01:48.729 --> 00:01:51.419
Now line up the phases at angle zero.

00:01:51.419 --> 00:01:53.895
The mean reaches one at the right endpoint.

00:01:53.895 --> 00:01:56.754
The supremum is exactly one, in every degree.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.205
Start with a sine wave.

00:01:59.205 --> 00:02:03.670
Multiply it by the Legendre value, and take k to be a positive integer.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.915
Sigma is a signed integral.

00:02:05.915 --> 00:02:10.826
Integrate P sub n of cosine t times sine of k t, from zero to pi.

00:02:11.156 --> 00:02:14.230
The Legendre factor has magnitude at most one.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:18.219
The product stays between the upper and lower absolute sine envelopes.

00:02:18.549 --> 00:02:21.346
Positive and negative areas can cancel.

00:02:21.346 --> 00:02:26.082
The magnitude of the signed integral is at most the integral of the absolute product.

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.291
Look at k equal to three.

00:02:28.291 --> 00:02:33.517
The upper envelope has three lobes, each of width pi over three and area two thirds.

00:02:33.847 --> 00:02:34.984
Count the lobes.

00:02:34.984 --> 00:02:40.206
For every positive integer k, the k lobes have area two over k each, totaling two.

00:02:40.536 --> 00:02:42.841
Every coefficient has magnitude at most two.

00:02:42.841 --> 00:02:46.702
This holds for every Legendre degree and every positive integer frequency.

